Read and write MP3s whose ID3v2 tag is followed by padding outside its size.

Why: 15 of 2,447 MP3s in the library carry zero bytes between the end of the
ID3v2 tag (its header size) and the first MPEG frame: 13 x 1044 bytes, one
1531, one 9405 (in-pod scan 2026-10-24). The picture reader guessed the
format from the bytes, and lofty's guess looks only 1024 bytes past the tag,
so these files failed with "no format could be determined" and Library
Management blocked them as FILE_UNREADABLE (10 in that day's dry run, one
taking its album along). The format is known from the file name, as for the
save's own read from the path: the picture reader now passes it. lofty's
write checks the type from the bytes again with the same 1024-byte reach,
so the ID3 save widens it to 64 KiB (7x the largest gap measured).

Tests: new test pads a fixture with 1044 zero bytes after its tag; it reads,
saves, keeps its pictures and its audio bytes. Reverting either half fails it
(read: picture reader; write: save refused). Full `cargo test --test library`
and `-p droppedneedle-library --lib`: same pre-existing macOS failure set as
without the series, one more test passing.

Remove when: upstream reads the format from the file it already knows.

